      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\), we need to determine how many times 3 appears as a factor in the numbers from 1 to 24. This is done by counting the multiples of 3, 9, 27 (since higher powers of 3 will exceed 24), and so on.\n\n1. Count the multiples of 3:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 8\n   \\]\n   So, there are 8 multiples of 3 in the numbers from 1 to 24.\n\n2. Count the multiples of 9:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{9} \\right\\rfloor = 2\n   \\]\n   So, there are 2 multiples of 9.\n\n3. Count the multiples of 27:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{27} \\right\\rfloor = 0\n   \\]\n   So, there are 0 multiples of 27.\n\nAdding these together gives the total number of factors of 3 in \\(24!\\):\n\\[\n8 + 2 + 0 = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
